<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Kasavubu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1915–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Joseph Kasa-Vubu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Kasavubu was the first president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a> (now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>), from independence in 1960 to 1965. As leader of ABAKO, a party of the Bakongo, he campaigned for independence. In office he clashed with prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a>, and he was deposed in Joseph-Désiré Mobutu's coup of November 1965.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-kasavubu.jpg" alt="Joseph Kasavubu" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown photographer / Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Kasa-Vubu_at_the_Belgo-Congolese_Round_Table_Conference.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Kasa-Vubu was born in the village of Kuma-Dizi in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayombe">Mayombe</a> district of Bas-Congo, in the west of the Belgian Congo, the eighth of nine children in a family of the Yombe, a subset of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> people. He was baptised Joseph in 1925 at the Scheutist Catholic mission of Kizu and studied at a minor seminary and then at the Kabwe seminary, intending to become a priest, but was expelled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1939. In 1941 he married Hortense Ngoma Masunda, with whom he had nine children. From 1942 he was a clerk in the finance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the Belgian colonial administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Léopoldville</a>, rising to chief clerk, the highest rank open to Congolese civil servants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kasa-Vubu began semi-clandestine political organising while still in colonial employment. In March 1954 he was elected president of the Bakongo Association (ABAKO), which swept the first open municipal elections in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Léopoldville</a> in 1957, and he became mayor of the Dendale district. He was among the first Congolese leaders to call for independence. On 4 January 1959 an ABAKO gathering he had organised, which the colonial authorities banned, erupted into the Léopoldville riots. He was arrested for inciting the riot and released two months later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In the 1960 elections ABAKO came third. Kasa-Vubu allied his regionalist, conservative party with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a>'s left-wing nationalist Congolese National Movement and was elected president indirectly, with Lumumba as prime minister. He was sworn in on 27 June 1960, and independence came on 30 June. Katanga and South Kasai soon seceded and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-crisis">Congo Crisis</a> began. When Lumumba sought help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, Kasa-Vubu accused him of communist sympathies and on 5 September dissolved his government. Lumumba was captured and killed by Katangese separatist forces in January 1961.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> support the secessions were put down by 1963, and the Kwilu and Simba rebellions were defeated by 1965. Kasa-Vubu named Moïse Tshombe prime minister in July 1964 and dismissed him in October 1965. On 25 November 1965 Joseph-Désiré Mobutu seized power and deposed him. Kasa-Vubu was placed under house arrest, retired to his farm in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayombe">Mayombe</a> and died in a hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boma">Boma</a> in 1969.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph Kasa-Vubu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Riots in Léopoldville in 1959 led Belgium to grant independence on 30 June 1960, with <b>Joseph Kasavubu</b> as president and Patrice Lumumba as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/belgian-congo">Belgian Congo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tshela, in what is now <a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Died in Boma. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-5,12.95&amp;year=1950" aria-label="Tshela on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4909 2350 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joseph Kasavubu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5359 2620h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5359 2620h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-5,12.95&amp;year=1950">What was here in 1950? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
